The code statements that lead to the bug are:
memcpy(dest, br_dev->dev_addr, ETH_ALEN);
Reason:
The `NFT_META_BRI_IIFHWADDR` case declares a destination register span of 8 bytes (two 32-bit registers), but the `memcpy` only writes 6 bytes (`ETH_ALEN`). The upper 2 bytes of the second register remain uninitialized from the `nft_do_chain()` stack, causing a stale stack leak when the full span is later read by userspace.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
dest[1] = 0;
memcpy(dest, br_dev->dev_addr, ETH_ALEN);
Reason:
Zeroing `dest[1]` (the second 32-bit register) before the `memcpy` ensures that the entire declared 8-byte register span is initialized. This prevents any uninitialized stack data from leaking to userspace during subsequent register reads.

